          "case": "nonempty-choice",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "No step invokes a choice principle: the finite-rank generating-function computation, the compatibility argument and the finite coefficient comparison use only finite sums and the exhibited bases. The Statement covers the nonempty-choice case with its exact hypotheses; none of them invokes a choice principle."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-forward",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The lemma is a single expansion identity, not an iff statement."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-reverse",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The lemma asserts no converse; the uniqueness of the coefficients is a property of the basis and is used, not proved as an iff clause."
        }
      ],
      "risk_review": {
        "status": "complete",
        "reviewer": "alpha-batch-18",
        "notes": "Proof 1.1–5.1 and all six dependencies reviewed. The formal exponential is used in the commutative rational t-adic power-series ring, with zero-constant-term arguments and finite contributions at each coefficient; exp(A+B)=exp(A)exp(B), not additivity. Rank projections give homogeneous degree d identities; p_k has degree k. Multiplying the one-row identities and collecting multiplicity matrices gives N(lambda,rho)/z_rho, with labelled rows and distinct equal-length cycles. Each partition is reconstructed in decreasing order. Empty matrices/products give N(empty,empty)=z_empty=h_empty=1; impossible equal-size distributions give zero without extending N to unequal sizes. Macdonald (2.14′) and its full proof, printed p. 25, agree. Pre/post item and contract hashes differ, while the current item equals post. The three reported proof corrections and corrected zero-boundary contract are sound; accepted without further item edits."
      }
    },
